## Motion, imagery, provider brands and frontend technology

### The motion system

I would use **four motion tiers**.

These values are recommended TrueCost starting tokens and should be tuned on actual devices:

| Token | Suggested duration | Use |
|---|---:|---|
| `instant` | 90–120 ms | icon/state response |
| `micro` | 120–180 ms | button/chip/press |
| `quick` | 180–240 ms | compact element |
| `standard` | 260–340 ms | sheet/card/content change |
| `expressive` | 380–520 ms | winner/search/major moment |

Google’s current Material motion system explicitly separates expressive motion for hero/prominent interactions from standard motion for utilitarian recurring interactions; Fluent likewise describes good motion as functional, natural, consistent and appealing, with choreography directing attention rather than simply making things move. citeturn8search5turn12search0

Recommended initial spring families:

```ts
export const motion = {
  press: {
    stiffness: 420,
    damping: 30,
    mass: 0.7,
  },

  card: {
    stiffness: 300,
    damping: 28,
    mass: 0.9,
  },

  hero: {
    stiffness: 220,
    damping: 24,
    mass: 1.0,
  },
} as const;
```

These are **design proposals**, not magic universal values.

The recommendation reveal should be:

```text
results softly appear
        ↓
prices resolve
        ↓
non-winners settle
        ↓
winner rises 4–6 px
        ↓
iris edge/halo appears
        ↓
"Save ₹116" appears
        ↓
one subtle haptic
        ↓
everything becomes still
```

That is more premium than endless looping glow.

For React Native, Reanimated is the strongest candidate for gesture-, layout- and scroll-driven premium interaction **if the project’s current React Native/Expo architecture is compatible with the current Reanimated 4 line**. Its current documentation recommends non-layout properties such as `transform` and `opacity` for performance and warns that excessive simultaneous component animation can reduce FPS, particularly on lower-end Android hardware. citeturn9search3turn9search6

React Native’s built-in `Animated` remains perfectly suitable for simpler transitions and can move supported animation work to native with `useNativeDriver`, particularly for transforms and opacity. citeturn13search0turn13search2

My stack recommendation is:

| Technology | TrueCost role | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| **Reanimated** | primary UI motion, gestures, scroll, winner interactions | **Primary** |
| React Native Animated | simple/legacy transitions | Keep where adequate |
| **Rive** | 1–3 signature branded interactive illustrations | Selective |
| Lottie | passive pre-authored illustration if already available | Secondary |
| Skia | genuinely custom data visualization/effects | Only when justified |
| Framer Motion | web frontend only | Not native-app core |

Rive is useful specifically for an owned `TrueCost Lens` because its state-machine runtime supports controlled state transitions and can stop advancing when it settles, avoiding perpetual unnecessary work. citeturn9search0turn9search8

### Reduced motion is mandatory

Premium motion must have two modes:

```text
FULL
REDUCED
```

Apple recommends reducing automatic/repetitive movement when Reduce Motion is active, tightening springs, replacing large spatial transitions with fades, and avoiding animated depth/blur changes. citeturn9search2

For example:

```text
Normal:
winner rises
+
background halo expands
+
price animates

Reduced motion:
winner crossfades
+
border/highlight appears
+
price updates immediately
```

### Images

Use images according to a hierarchy.

**Exact entity imagery**:

```text
Grocery product
Shopping product
Medicine packaging
Food item
Hotel/room if relevant
```

only when tied to that actual underlying entity.

**Editorial imagery**:

```text
Travel hero
Food category hero
Onboarding
Category atmosphere
```

does not pretend to depict the exact offer.

**TrueCost illustration**:

```text
No match
No providers available
Comparison completed
Privacy
Onboarding
Empty state
```

Expo Image is a strong production fit because its current implementation provides disk/memory caching, BlurHash/ThumbHash placeholders, source transitions and controlled content fitting/positioning. citeturn13search4

The desired image lifecycle is:

```text
known aspect ratio
        ↓
soft tonal placeholder
        ↓
BlurHash / ThumbHash
        ↓
real image crossfade
        ↓
cached high-resolution asset
```

Never:

```text
blank grey rectangle
        ↓
300 ms layout shift
        ↓
stretched image
```

### Real provider logos

Yes: **use real provider identities where legally permitted and properly sourced.**

But build this as a system, not random files copied from search results.

```ts
type ProviderBrand = {
  id: string;
  displayName: string;

  logoAsset?: ImageSourcePropType;
  logoSource?: string;

  legalStatus:
    | 'approved'
    | 'official-public-asset'
    | 'review-required'
    | 'text-fallback';

  lightSurfaceAsset?: ImageSourcePropType;
  darkSurfaceAsset?: ImageSourcePropType;

  minimumClearSpace?: number;
  preferredBackground?: 'light' | 'dark' | 'either';

  lastReviewedAt?: string;
};
```

For each of your 32 providers, the asset registry should record:

| Category | Provider | Official asset located | Usage terms reviewed | Light asset | Fallback |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Grocery | Blinkit | Audit | Audit | Audit | `Blinkit` text |
| Grocery | Zepto | Audit | Audit | Audit | `Zepto` text |
| Grocery | Instamart | Audit | Audit | Audit | `Instamart` text |
| Grocery | BigBasket | Audit | Audit | Audit | `BigBasket` text |
| Food | Swiggy | Audit | Audit | Audit | `Swiggy` text |
| Food | Zomato | Audit | Audit | Audit | `Zomato` text |
| … | all remaining providers | Audit | Audit | Audit | provider name |

Do not assume that because a logo is publicly visible it can be reused arbitrarily. Amazon’s published brand-use guidance, for example, restricts ordinary Amazon-logo usage in various third-party contexts and requires approval in some situations. citeturn10search1

Most importantly:

> **Animate the TrueCost container around the logo—not another company’s trademark itself.**

Good:

```text
provider tile scales 0.97 → 1
logo remains unchanged
```

Bad:

```text
Amazon logo spins
Slack logo changes color
Zomato logo stretches
Uber mark gains glowing purple stroke
```

### Haptics

Use haptics for:

```text
important selection
basket optimization complete
winner confirmation
successful save
meaningful warning/error
```

Not every tap.

Expo currently exposes selection, impact and notification-style haptic feedback on Android and iOS, making this practical for a React Native/Expo application. citeturn13search3

## The implementation program I recommend

Do **not** give a coding agent the instruction:

> “Make every page beautiful.”

That almost guarantees inconsistent colors, duplicate cards and animations invented screen by screen.

Do this as a product-design program.

