### Hard UI truth gates

The UI should **never** become visually “better” by weakening backend truth.

Your hard release counters should be:

```text
UI_WRONG_WINNER_COUNT = 0

UI_WRONG_PRICE_COUNT = 0

UI_WRONG_VARIANT_COUNT = 0

UI_WRONG_QUANTITY_COUNT = 0

REJECTED_SHOWN_AS_EXACT = 0

PROBABLE_SHOWN_AS_EXACT = 0

UNKNOWN_RENDERED_AS_ZERO = 0

STALE_DATA_SHOWN_AS_LIVE = 0

PROVIDER_COVERAGE_MISREPRESENTATION = 0

UNAUTHORIZED_PROVIDER_BRAND_ASSET = 0

P0_UI_ISSUES = 0
```

This is particularly important given the current repository state: some categories currently have substantially stronger real evidence/backend completeness than others. The redesigned UI should show beautiful unavailable/partial states rather than using synthetic content to make every category appear equally live. fileciteturn0file4

### Accessibility targets

A world-class redesign is not elite if it fails at larger type sizes or relies on animation/color alone.

Apple recommends support for larger text, VoiceOver-compatible interfaces and reduced-motion behavior; Apple’s current accessibility guidance also notes 44×44 pt as its standard iOS control size, while Android guidance generally uses 48dp touch targets. citeturn9search2turn9search4

For TrueCost I would standardize interactive mobile hit areas to **at least 48×48 logical units where practical**, even if the visible icon is smaller.

Every state such as:

```text
BEST
STALE
UNAVAILABLE
UNKNOWN
EXACT
```

needs both text/icon/shape meaning, not color alone. Apple explicitly recommends supplementing color with distinct indicators for accessibility. citeturn9search2

### Loading states

Do not use one spinner for the entire app.

A loading skeleton should resemble the structure that will replace it, which is also the pattern Fluent recommends for shimmer loading states; its guidance cautions against indefinite shimmer for long-running operations and disables its animation under reduced-motion settings. citeturn12search1turn12search7

For example:

```text
Recommendation loading:

╭────────────────────────────────╮
│ ▓▓▓▓                           │
│                                │
│ ▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓                       │
│                                │
│       ▓▓▓▓▓                    │
│                                │
│ ▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓                   │
╰────────────────────────────────╯
```

Then transition into the real hierarchy rather than jumping layouts.

### Migration without breaking backend truth

The migration should be layered:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Existing Backend Contracts] --> B[Frontend Adapter Layer]
    B --> C[Design-System Components]
    C --> D[Domain Presentation]
    D --> E[Motion / Imagery]
    E --> F[UI Certification]

    A -. do not rewrite for aesthetics .-> A
```

The implementation order I would enforce is:

```text
Keep backend contracts
        ↓
Introduce tokens
        ↓
Introduce primitives
        ↓
Wrap current screens
        ↓
Replace duplicated UI with shared components
        ↓
Redesign Home/Search
        ↓
Redesign Recommendation/Comparison
        ↓
Grocery
        ↓
Shopping
        ↓
Mobility/Travel
        ↓
remaining domains
        ↓
motion
        ↓
accessibility
        ↓
performance
        ↓
before/after screenshot QA
```

Do not rewrite the Entity Resolution engine because a variant badge looks inconvenient.

Do not convert `null` into `0` because the card looks nicer with a number.

Do not show a fake provider because four logos make a composition more symmetrical.

Do not enable synchronous provider fetching because the loading animation looks better when fresh results arrive.

### Post-release metrics

The final redesign should be judged by behavior, not screenshots alone.

Track:

| Metric | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| Time to first useful result | speed of value |
| Time to identify the recommended option | hierarchy effectiveness |
| Search → comparison completion | journey quality |
| Comparison → provider action | decision usefulness |
| Search refinement rate | intent/search quality |
| No-result recovery rate | empty-state effectiveness |
| Backtracking rate | navigation confusion |
| “Why this won” open rate | trust/explanation usage |
| Provider-coverage disclosure interactions | uncertainty comprehension |
| Wrong-price incidents | accuracy |
| Wrong-variant incidents | identity safety |
| Wrong-winner incidents | critical correctness |
| Crash-free sessions | stability |
| p95 screen-render latency | perceived responsiveness |
| image-load latency | imagery quality |
| dropped/janky frame rate during hero transitions | motion quality |
| accessibility defects | inclusivity |
| reduced-motion correctness | accessibility |
| provider-logo fallback frequency | asset completeness |

### The final recommendation

I would make the TrueCost redesign around **five signature ideas**:

**The Search Lens** — an unusually beautiful universal “ask TrueCost anything” entry point.

**The Recommendation Hero** — the user instantly sees what won and why.

**The Comparison Language** — every category shares the same conceptual logic while rendering domain-specific details.

**The TrueCost Lens Motion** — one subtle, ownable visual symbol for understanding → comparing → decision.

**Porcelain Intelligence** — white, calm, expensive surfaces with carefully rationed iris, mint, glass, imagery and motion.

The final screen hierarchy should feel like:

```text
               WHAT YOU ASKED

                      ↓

              WHAT WE COMPARED

                      ↓

               ✦ WHAT WON ✦

                      ↓

                 WHY IT WON

                      ↓

            WHAT THE TRADEOFF IS

                      ↓

          WHAT DATA WE ACTUALLY KNOW
```

That structure directly supports the principles Apple now emphasizes—purpose, simplicity, responsibility, craft and delight—while Material and Fluent support the use of expressive motion, semantic tokens, hierarchy and platform-appropriate behavior. citeturn10search3turn8search5turn12search0turn12search2

The goal is **not** to make someone say “wow, lots of effects.”

The goal is:

> **“Wow, this looks unbelievably polished—and I understood the best decision almost instantly.”**

That is the direction I would choose for TrueCost.